---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
import portrait from '../assets/CarmenKrol.jpg';

const description =
  'A decade of software engineering, including nearly five years leading ' +
  'accessibility work at Meta across web, Android and iOS.';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={description}>
  <div class="page region">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Carmen Krol</h1>

    <div class="prose about">
      <div class="about__intro">
        {/* Decorative: the h1 immediately above already names her, so
            describing the portrait would announce "Carmen Krol" twice. */}
        {/* Fixed display width, so densities rather than widths/sizes: it
            emits 1x and 2x only, and keeps src on the 220px copy instead of
            falling back to the 2908px original. */}
        <Image
          src={portrait}
          alt=""
          class="about__portrait"
          width={220}
          densities={[1, 2]}
          loading="eager"
        />
        <p class="about__lede">
          I’m a software engineer with more than a decade of experience building
          user interfaces, including nearly five years dedicated to
          accessibility at Meta.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        I’ve worked with product teams to understand accessibility requirements,
        solve implementation challenges, and make accessibility a more practical
        part of software development.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>
        I enjoy the intersection of accessibility and engineering: understanding
        why something doesn’t work for someone using assistive technology,
        figuring out how to fix it, and helping prevent similar problems from
        happening again.
      </p>
      <p>
        My experience spans web, Android, and iOS. I’ve worked with engineers
        and product teams to address accessibility issues, contributed to
        accessible components and design systems, and built tools to make
        accessibility testing and remediation more effective.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m particularly interested in the practical questions that come up
        during development: How should this component behave? What does a screen
        reader need to communicate? Which accessibility requirements can be
        addressed in the code, and how can teams catch problems earlier?
      </p>
      <p>
        More recently, I’ve also worked on AI-based approaches to identifying
        and addressing accessibility issues. I’m interested in what AI can make
        easier for accessibility practitioners and software engineers—and where
        AI-generated software still needs a human accessibility perspective.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        I believe accessibility works best when it’s part of how software is
        built, rather than something added at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means combining automated tools with manual testing, understanding
        the implementation decisions behind accessibility issues, and helping
        engineers understand why something needs to change—not just what needs
        to change. The goal is not simply to fix one accessibility issue, but to
        make it easier for teams to build accessible experiences the next time
        around.
      </p>

      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>
        I was most recently a tech lead on Meta’s central accessibility team,
        where I worked with product teams across web, Android, and iOS. My work
        included accessibility remediation, accessible components and design
        systems, testing tools, and AI-based approaches to accessibility.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m now taking time to explore independent consulting and other
        opportunities to apply my engineering and accessibility experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before specializing in accessibility, I worked as a software engineer
        across a variety of products and teams. I also hold a{' '}
        <a
          href="https://www.accessibilityassociation.org/cpacc"
          rel="noopener"
          target="_blank"
        >
          CPACC certification from the International Association of
          Accessibility Professionals (IAAP)
        </a>{' '}
        and a master’s degree in Science &amp; Technology Studies from Cornell
        University.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={site.linkedInUrl} rel="noopener" target="_blank">
            LinkedIn
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={site.gitHubUrl} rel="noopener" target="_blank">
            GitHub
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The quickest way to reach me is <a href="/contact/">the contact form</a>
        , or <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> if you would
        rather email.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Wraps on its own once the two items no longer fit, so the portrait
     stacks above the text on narrow screens without a breakpoint. */
  .about__intro {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: start;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  .about__portrait {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    inline-size: 220px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .about__lede {
    /* min-inline-size: 0 stops the flex item refusing to shrink below the
       width of its longest word, which is what causes overflow at 320px. */
    flex: 1 1 22ch;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .about h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }
</style>
